WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:10.608
From nineteen fifty-nine, British Rail ordered hundreds of locomotives powered by a single engine: the Sulzer twelve L D A twenty-eight.

2
00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:19.437
It hauled the Peaks, the Class forty-four, forty-five and forty-six, and the Brush Type Four, now known as the Class forty-seven.

3
00:00:19.437 --> 00:00:20.874
Here is how it works.

4
00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:24.397
Twelve cylinders, but not in a V.

5
00:00:24.397 --> 00:00:30.169
It is two separate inline sixes in one welded frame, each with its own crankshaft.

6
00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:35.237
Bore two hundred and eighty millimetres, stroke three hundred and sixty.

7
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:43.262
About twenty-two litres a cylinder, and roughly two hundred and sixty-six in all, calculated from bore and stroke.

8
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:53.736
The two crankshafts are tied together by synchronising gears, which step the speed up by one point four four to drive the main generator.

9
00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:56.782
The generator feeds electric traction motors.

10
00:00:56.782 --> 00:00:59.219
The engine never touches the wheels.

11
00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:02.929
Pull it apart and the layers appear.

12
00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:13.945
Crankcase, blocks and liners, heads with their valves, pistons and connecting rods, camshafts, injection pumps, and the turbochargers and intercoolers on top.

13
00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:19.823
Each cylinder works in four strokes over two turns of the crankshaft.

14
00:01:19.823 --> 00:01:25.722
Intake: the piston falls and the inlet valve admits air, pushed in by the turbocharger.

15
00:01:25.722 --> 00:01:36.232
Compression: both valves shut, and the rising piston squeezes the air to a small fraction of its volume, heating it far above the ignition point of diesel.

16
00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:43.216
Power: fuel is injected just before top dead centre and burns, and the pressure drives the piston down.

17
00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:48.233
Exhaust: the exhaust valve opens, and the piston sweeps out the burnt gas.

18
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:54.429
The valve timing on this site is an engineering estimate, not Sulzer data.

19
00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:56.792
We could not find a primary source.

20
00:01:56.792 --> 00:02:02.935
Inlet and exhaust overlap around top dead centre, so that boost air can flush the cylinder.

21
00:02:04.135 --> 00:02:14.441
Twelve cylinders share seven hundred and twenty degrees, so on average a power stroke begins every sixty degrees, if the firing is evenly spaced.

22
00:02:14.441 --> 00:02:23.680
At full speed, seven hundred and fifty revolutions a minute, that is seventy-five firings every second: a seventy-five hertz note.

23
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.523
The firing order shown is an assumption.

24
00:02:26.523 --> 00:02:28.655
No source we reached gives it.

25
00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:39.687
Exhaust gas spins a de Havilland turbocharger, which compresses the air to roughly two bar absolute on the Peaks, sixteen to eighteen pounds gauge.

26
00:02:39.687 --> 00:02:44.904
On the B and C engines, an intercooler cools that air, packing in more oxygen.

27
00:02:44.904 --> 00:02:51.525
The A, in the Class forty-four, had no intercooler, which is the likely reason it makes less power.

28
00:02:51.525 --> 00:02:56.675
That is an inference from the six-cylinder engine, not stated for the twelve.

29
00:02:57.875 --> 00:03:09.213
The sound comes from the same machinery: the thump of each firing, the exhaust puffs, the valve ticks, the turbocharger whine rising with load, and the hum of the gear train.

30
00:03:09.213 --> 00:03:14.686
The synthesiser on the site builds the note from those events, cylinder by cylinder.

31
00:03:15.886 --> 00:03:16.853
Three ratings.

32
00:03:16.853 --> 00:03:21.687
The A: two thousand three hundred horsepower, in the Class forty-four.

33
00:03:21.687 --> 00:03:30.526
The B: two thousand five hundred at seven hundred and fifty revolutions, in Classes forty-five and forty-six, with intercooling.

34
00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:43.854
The C, in the Class forty-seven, was built for two thousand seven hundred and fifty at eight hundred, and later derated to about two thousand five hundred and eighty at seven hundred and fifty.

35
00:03:45.054 --> 00:03:46.581
Power rises with speed.

36
00:03:46.581 --> 00:03:54.017
The rated points are sourced; the curve between idle and full speed is a modelled shape, and is labelled as one.

37
00:03:54.017 --> 00:03:58.266
As the driver opens the controller, the engine climbs this line.

38
00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:05.852
The driver's power controller has positions off, on, quarter, half, three quarters and full.

39
00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:10.780
The governor turns that demand into fuel, and the engine speed follows.

40
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:15.291
Idle is about three hundred and twenty-five revolutions a minute.

41
00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:27.399
At the rail, a Class forty-seven gives sixty-two thousand pounds of tractive effort at the start, and thirty thousand pounds continuous at twenty-six miles an hour.

42
00:04:27.399 --> 00:04:31.324
Roughly four-fifths of the engine's power reaches the rail.

43
00:04:31.324 --> 00:04:33.186
That figure is our estimate.

44
00:04:34.386 --> 00:04:39.736
From fuel to rail: engine, generator, six traction motors, gears and wheels.

45
00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:45.439
An electric transmission, which is why one diesel engine can start a heavy train.

46
00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:53.119
Every figure on the site carries a badge: well sourced, single source, weak source, or estimate.

47
00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:57.169
The best sources were the British Railways driver's manuals.

48
00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:04.391
The gaps, such as valve timing, firing order, compression ratio and peak pressure, are marked as estimates.

49
00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.119
Start it, slow it down, pull it apart, and listen.

50
00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:12.224
It is all on the Sulzer page of the almanac.

51
00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:13.636
Thanks for watching.
